s("How can I sell my house fast in Concord, NC?",
"<p>You can list with an agent, sell on your own, or take a cash offer from a buyer who purchases as-is. Concord sellers choose between them mostly on timing, repair needs and how much they want to handle themselves.</p>"
"<p>A traditional listing can bring the highest price but asks you to prepare the house, allow showings and wait out the buyer's loan approval. A cash sale trades some of that price for speed and certainty. Concord sits in Cabarrus County, north and east of Charlotte, so many buyers here are already comparing the two metros.</p>"
"<ul><li>List with an agent when the house is in good shape and you can wait for a conventional closing.</li>"
"<li>Sell on your own if you are comfortable pricing, marketing and handling the contract.</li>"
"<li>Take a cash offer if the house needs work, is inherited, has tenants, or you need a firm closing date.</li></ul>"
"<p>Whichever route you take, the deed is recorded with the Cabarrus County Register of Deeds, and North Carolina law makes the recording date what counts against other buyers and lien creditors.</p>")
s("What is Concord like for someone selling a house?",
"<p>Concord is the largest city in Cabarrus County and its county seat, with a Census Bureau estimate of 114,598 residents on July 1, 2025. The city was founded in 1796 on a 26 acre site after the area's German and Scots-Irish settlements compromised over where the new county's seat should go. The name Concord, meaning harmony, records that settlement.</p>"
"<p>The Census estimate is about 8.8 percent above the April 2020 estimates base, so the city keeps adding households. The 2020 Census counted 105,240 people, up from 79,066 in 2010. Textile history shaped the older neighborhoods: the city's own history page points to the Cannon Mills founder's 1928 house on Union Street North as one landmark.</p>"
"<table><caption>Concord city facts from Census QuickFacts</caption><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Concord figure</th></tr></thead><tbody>"
"<tr><td>Population estimate, July 1, 2025</td><td>114,598</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Owner-occupied housing unit rate, 2020-2024</td><td>65.2%</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Median value of owner-occupied homes, 2020-2024</td><td>$353,700</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Median gross rent, 2020-2024</td><td>$1,451</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Households, 2020-2024</td><td>38,669</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Mean travel time to work</td><td>26.9 minutes</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Land area, 2020</td><td>63.48 square miles</td></tr></tbody></table>"
"<p>Roughly two in three homes are owner occupied, which tells a Concord seller that most buyers in a typical sale are moving in rather than renting the place out. The 26.9 minute average commute reflects how many residents work elsewhere in the Charlotte area.</p>")
s("Who sets and collects property tax on a Concord house?",
"<p>Cabarrus County values the property and collects the bill, and the City of Concord adds its own rate. The county tax office collects for the county, seven cities or towns and 16 fire districts, and each rate is set in the budget process by June 30.</p>"
"<p>The county's Tax Revaluation page lists what feeds a parcel's value: land (location, size and zoning), the building (type, age and physical features), and extras such as garages, sheds, pools, fences and driveways. Appeals page language says the 2024 revaluation used a January 1, 2024 effective date. Ask the Tax Administration office for the current rate on your parcel, because the combined figure depends on your city and fire district.</p>"
"<p>North Carolina bills taxes for the fiscal year starting July 1, with payment due September 1. A bill paid before January 6 carries no interest. After that, interest runs at 2 percent for the period to February 1, then three-fourths of one percent for each month or fraction after that.</p>"
"<ul><li>Listing period: the first business day of January through January 31.</li>"
"<li>Bills: due September 1, interest starts on unpaid bills after January 5.</li>"
"<li>Tax lien: attaches to the parcel as of the January listing date, so a purchaser inherits any unpaid year.</li></ul>")
s("How do I appeal my Concord property value?",
"<p>Start with the informal review in the Cabarrus County Tax Revaluation office, then move to the Board of Equalization and Review if you still disagree. Your appeal must reach the county before that board adjourns, which the county says usually happens in late April.</p>"
"<p>Informal review runs from January until the board convenes, which state law places no earlier than the first Monday in April and no later than the first Monday in May. You can file in writing at the Cabarrus County Government Center, first floor, 65 Church Street S, Concord, by mail to PO Box 707, Concord NC 28026-0707, or through the online system with supporting documents attached. If a Notice of Real Estate Assessed Value arrives, you have 30 days from the date on it.</p>"
"<ol><li>Informal review with an appraiser.</li><li>Board of Equalization and Review, whose members the county commissioners appoint.</li>"
"<li>North Carolina Property Tax Commission, with an application due within 30 days of the board's decision notice.</li>"
"<li>Court of Appeals and Supreme Court, where a North Carolina licensed attorney must represent you.</li></ol>"
"<p>The Revaluation office answers questions at 704-920-2126, Monday through Friday from 8:00 AM to 5:00 PM.</p>")
s("What relief programs can lower a Concord homeowner's tax?",
"<p>North Carolina offers three programs, and an owner can receive only one of them. They are the elderly or disabled exclusion, the disabled veteran exclusion and the circuit breaker deferral, and the county assessor takes the application.</p>"
"<ul><li>Elderly or disabled exclusion: the greater of $25,000 or 50 percent of the home's appraised value, for owners 65 or older or totally and permanently disabled, with prior-year income under the limit. NCDOR prints that limit as $38,800 for the 2026 tax year.</li>"
"<li>Disabled veteran exclusion: the first $45,000 of appraised value, with no age or income limit printed on the NCDOR form.</li>"
"<li>Circuit breaker: tax capped at 4 percent of income at or under the limit, or 5 percent above it up to 150 percent of the limit ($58,200 for 2026), with an excess that is deferred.</li></ul>"
"<p>File on Form AV-9 with the Cabarrus County assessor, ideally during the January listing period. The county accepts applications through June 1 before the tax year. The circuit breaker needs a new application every year, and deferred tax for the prior three years comes due if the home is transferred.</p>")
s("What does it cost to transfer a house in Concord at closing?",
"<p>The seller pays the deed excise tax to the Cabarrus County Register of Deeds before the deed is recorded. State law sets it at $1.00 for each $500, or fractional part, of the price or value conveyed.</p>"
"<p>Half of the excise tax stays with the county general fund and half goes to the state. The recording fees are separate and uniform across the state: $26.00 for an ordinary instrument of up to 15 pages with $4.00 for each additional page, and $64.00 for a deed of trust of up to 35 pages. Satisfactions of an existing deed of trust record with no fee.</p>"
"<p>Property tax for the year is usually prorated between buyer and seller by the closing attorney. Because the lien dates to January, ask the Cabarrus County collector for a payoff figure on the parcel before closing so nothing surprises you at the table.</p>")
s("Which Concord rules and permits affect a sale?",
"<p>The City of Concord Development Ordinance controls what you may build or change, and it requires permits and approvals before work begins. A house with unpermitted additions can slow a sale, so gather permit records before you list or accept an offer.</p>"
"<p>The ordinance has a residential use type for accessory dwelling units, also called carriage houses, and it sets parking for each unit. Short-term rentals appear in the ordinance under household living, so confirm with the city planning department whether a particular house may be rented nightly before you market it to an investor. Concord's historic-district rules appear in its ordinance as well, and a house inside one may need extra approval for exterior changes.</p>"
"<p>Concord's Historic Facts page lists the Cannon house on Union Street North, built in 1928, and the 1924 building that became the Old Courthouse Theater in 1986. Owners of older homes near downtown should ask the planning staff what review applies.</p>")
s("What if my Concord house is inherited, in foreclosure or rented?",
"<p>Each of these situations has a state-law clock, and a cash buyer can often fit inside it. Read the paperwork you received and note the dates before you accept an offer.</p>"
"<ul><li>Foreclosure: North Carolina uses a hearing before the clerk of superior court in the county where the land lies. After a sale, anyone can file an upset bid within 10 days that exceeds the last bid by at least 5 percent with a $750 minimum.</li>"
"<li>Inherited property: a small intestate estate under $20,000 may use an affidavit after 30 days from the date of death. Larger estates need a personal representative. North Carolina repealed its estate tax for deaths on or after January 1, 2013.</li>"
"<li>Tenants: a month-to-month tenancy ends on 7 days' notice, and nonpayment gives the landlord a right to act after 10 days from demand.</li></ul>"
"<p>If a tenant's lease continues, tell any buyer, because a sale does not end a lease by itself in most cases. Ask a North Carolina attorney about how your particular lease reads.</p>")
